# Copy to .env and edit. Set WORKER and, if discovery is ambiguous, fabric overrides.

# ssh destination of the second Spark (rank 1). Passwordless ssh, and its user
# can run docker. Its address on the direct cable makes the one-time copy of
# the ~180 GB of weights much faster.
WORKER=you@192.0.2.2

# Where the checkpoint and the drafter live (~180 GB). The same absolute path is
# used on both Sparks. Default: $HOME/models/sparkglm
#MODEL_ROOT=/srv/models/sparkglm

# 4x1m (default): 4 requests x 1M context, the model's full window; the
# requests share one KV pool (~2.5-2.7M tokens with the shard, the carveout and
# the disk prefix cache at 0.91). 4x512k: 4 x 512K. 8x128k: 8 x 128K.
#PROFILE=4x1m

# TP2 precision policy: strict preserves checkpoint weight formats and uses
# serial A16 decode. spark adds corrected FP4 KDA decode and the tested
# speculative performance profile. Both ranks must use the same mode.
#MODE=strict

# Share of each Spark's unified memory for the engine (0.80-0.95, default 0.88).
# On Sparks that run nothing else, 0.91 roughly doubles the KV pool that holds
# long contexts and the prefix cache (about 600K to 1.13M tokens unsplit; the
# KV shard below holds about 1.8x that). Above 0.91,
# real traffic took our rank 0 below 1 GiB free; keep at least ~4 GB free.
#GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.91

# Keep conversations that fall out of the prefix cache on each Spark's own disk
# (ext4/xfs, not tmpfs), so a returning conversation is read back instead of
# prefilled again. Off unless the directory is set. It is created at the same
# path on both Sparks. The size is in GiB per Spark (16-100, default 48) and
# must be free there; the default takes about 60K tokens from an unsplit KV
# pool, about 250K from a split one (2.61M to 2.36M at 0.91 with the carveout).
#PREFIX_CACHE_DIR=/srv/sparkglm/prefix-cache
#PREFIX_CACHE_GB=48

# Serve another checkpoint on the same image: both values or neither. The engine
# also serves NVIDIA's earlier NVFP4 release (190 GiB, the default until
# 2026-10-17), through the MTP overlay ./start.sh converts on each Spark (16 GB):
#CHECKPOINT_REPO=nvidia/GLM-5.3-Flash-NVFP4
#CHECKPOINT_REVISION=423acf37583782c51c142d145aef733d72943d93

# Lend each Spark's 2 GiB display carveout (GB10 firmware memory that Linux
# never sees) to the KV cache. The containers share /run/lock/sparkglm.
# Default off; set 0 or delete the line to roll back.
#DISPLAY_CARVEOUT=1

# The KV cache is split between the two Sparks: each stores the latents of half
# the blocks, so the shared pool holds about 1.8x the tokens. With the display
# carveout and the disk prefix cache at 0.91: ~2.66M tokens with FP4-Local
# (2.34M with the NVFP4 checkpoint), lowest free memory 3.3 GB in a
# four-session 204K fill. On by default, with or without the disk prefix
# cache (each Spark spills only its own half). 0 turns it off.
#KV_SHARD=0

# Build the image from source instead of pulling ghcr.io/enntity/atlas-sparkglm.
#BUILD=1
# Parallel compile jobs for a source build (default: one per ~10 GiB of free
# memory, at most half the cores); lower it if the build runs out of memory.
#ATLAS_BUILD_JOBS=4
# Skip the in-image release checks (default 1: run them) for an engine commit
# that already passed them; the image itself is the same either way.
#ATLAS_IMAGE_CHECKS=0
# Operational flags survive the launcher's ATLAS_* strip, because they change
# observability or recovery, not numerics: ATLAS_LOG_* knobs and the #55
# loop-suppression escape hatch. The engine still reports it; masking
# tool-call syntax on repeated short turns is off with ATLAS_LOOP_NO_SUPPRESS=1.

# Only for private Hugging Face mirrors; the pinned checkpoints are public.
#HF_TOKEN=

# Resolve each node's active RDMA interface independently. With no overrides,
# exactly one matching IPv4 subnet pair is required; ambiguous discovery stops.
# ./start.sh fabric checks selection without Docker, downloads or model changes.
# Override each host independently (the same physical port number is not required).
# List ALL intended rails, in matching peer order; extra siblings are not added.
#LEADER_FABRIC_HCA=rocep1s0f1,roceP2p1s0f1
#WORKER_FABRIC_HCA=rocep1s0f0,roceP2p1s0f0
#LEADER_FABRIC_INTERFACE=enp1s0f1np1
#WORKER_FABRIC_INTERFACE=enp1s0f0np0
# FABRIC_HCA / FABRIC_INTERFACE remain shared fallbacks for either unset side.
# LEADER_ADDRESS, if set, must belong to the selected leader interface.
#LEADER_ADDRESS=192.0.2.1

# Tool-call guard: on by default in both Strict and Spark modes.
# Blocks metadata probes, listed callback hosts and recognizable credential transfers.
# Exact host exceptions do not override metadata protection or explicit denials.
#TOOL_GUARD=on
#TOOL_GUARD_ALLOW_HOSTS=uploads.example.org
#TOOL_GUARD_DENY_HOSTS=blocked.example.org
#TOOL_GUARD_MAX_BYTES=1048576
# See docs/TOOL-GUARD.md for coverage and limits. This is not a network sandbox.

# GPU clock cap. ./start.sh clock-cap installs gpu-clock-cap.service on both
# Sparks (sudo), which locks the GPU clock to 300..GPU_CLOCK_MAX_MHZ at every
# boot. Uncapped, a long cold prefill can power a Spark off. ./start.sh refuses
# to serve until both Sparks run it; CLOCK_CAP=0 skips that check.
#GPU_CLOCK_MAX_MHZ=2200
#CLOCK_CAP=0
